 ### Palette-based Alpha Cutoff
-Applies a hard cutoff to sampled volume values before palette mapping, discarding values outside a specified range and scaling the remaining alpha [S3, unofficial]. It computes alpha using two step functions: $\alpha = \text{clamp}(\text{step}(minCutoffValue, meanValue) \times \text{step}(meanValue, maxCutoffValue) \times alphaMultiplier, 0.0, 1.0)$ [S3, unofficial]. The RGB color is sampled from a horizontal palette texture at the normalized mean value [S3, unofficial]. Parameters include `minCutoffValue` (default $10^{-3}$), `maxCutoffValue` (default $1 - 10^{-3}$), and `alphaMultiplier` (default $1.0$) [S3, unofficial].
+Applies a hard cutoff to sampled volume values before palette mapping, discarding values outside a specified range and scaling the remaining alpha [S4, unofficial]. It computes alpha using two step functions: $\alpha = \text{clamp}(\text{step}(minCutoffValue, meanValue) \times \text{step}(meanValue, maxCutoffValue) \times alphaMultiplier, 0.0, 1.0)$ [S4, unofficial]. The RGB color is sampled from a horizontal palette texture at the normalized mean value [S4, unofficial]. Parameters include `minCutoffValue` (default $10^{-3}$), `maxCutoffValue` (default $1 - 10^{-3}$), and `alphaMultiplier` (default $1.0$) [S4, unofficial].
 
 ### VNetL-16-4
-An architecture creating 16 semantic channels in the encoder, mapped to color and opacity via an MLP-based TF [S4, unofficial]. It replaces the VNet encoder with VNetLight (one less downsampling level) to produce 16 semantic channels [S4, unofficial]. The color TF is an MLP with a 16-channel hidden layer (ReLU) producing 3 RGB channels (sigmoid) [S4, unofficial]. The layout is Encoder: VNet16, TF: MLP1, Accumulation: Alpha, Decoder: Identity [S4, unofficial].
+An architecture creating 16 semantic channels in the encoder, mapped to color and opacity via an MLP-based TF [S5, unofficial]. It replaces the VNet encoder with VNetLight (one less downsampling level) to produce 16 semantic channels [S5, unofficial]. The color TF is an MLP with a 16-channel hidden layer (ReLU) producing 3 RGB channels (sigmoid) [S5, unofficial]. The layout is Encoder: VNet16, TF: MLP1, Accumulation: Alpha, Decoder: Identity [S5, unofficial].
 
 ### Dynamic Opacity via Specular Highlights
-A technique using dynamic opacity as a function of specular highlights to allow additional information in partially transparent parts, leveraging the human visual system's ability to complete shapes [S5, unofficial]. It excludes certain data-value ranges from transparency modulation to allow clear views of inner structures [S5, unofficial]. Parameters include the Specularity/Transparency Ratio and Depth (fuzzy clipping) [S5, unofficial].
+A technique using dynamic opacity as a function of specular highlights to allow additional information in partially transparent parts, leveraging the human visual system's ability to complete shapes [S6, unofficial]. It excludes certain data-value ranges from transparency modulation to allow clear views of inner structures [S6, unofficial]. Parameters include the Specularity/Transparency Ratio and Depth (fuzzy clipping) [S6, unofficial].
 
 ### Specular Highlight Augmentation
-Selectively boosts opacity in regions of high specular highlight to mitigate the loss of specular contribution due to low opacity [S6]. It calculates the specular contribution ($spec = \text{pow}(\text{dot}(\text{lightDir}, \text{normal}), \text{shininess})$) and updates sample alpha: $\alpha_{sample} = \alpha_{base} + spec \times \mu_{spec}$ [S6]. The parameter $\mu_{spec}$ depends on surface thickness, base opacity, and ray-stepping size [S6].
+Selectively boosts opacity in regions of high specular highlight to mitigate the loss of specular contribution due to low opacity [S7]. It calculates the specular contribution ($spec = \text{pow}(\text{dot}(\text{lightDir}, \text{normal}), \text{shininess})$) and updates sample alpha: $\alpha_{sample} = \alpha_{base} + spec \times \mu_{spec}$ [S7]. The parameter $\mu_{spec}$ depends on surface thickness, base opacity, and ray-stepping size [S7].
 
 ### Gradient/Angle-based MIP Transfer Function
-Assigns optical properties based on gradient magnitude and the angle between the viewing direction and the gradient vector, using Maximum Intensity Projection (MIP) compositing [S7, unofficial]. It avoids complex transfer functions by deriving opacity from geometric cues [S7, unofficial]. Optional level lines may be rendered to improve spatial perception [S7, unofficial].
+Assigns optical properties based on gradient magnitude and the angle between the viewing direction and the gradient vector, using Maximum Intensity Projection (MIP) compositing [S8, unofficial]. It avoids complex transfer functions by deriving opacity from geometric cues [S8, unofficial]. Optional level lines may be rendered to improve spatial perception [S8, unofficial].
+
+### Perceptually Linear Transfer Function for Order Independent Operators
+Adapts transfer functions for order-independent volume rendering (summation and MIP) by applying the inverse JND curve, ensuring perceptual linearity [S9]. For the summation operator, changes contribute linearly to pixel intensity, while for MIP, changes are linear if the modified value is the maximum along the viewing ray [S9]. Unlike direct volume rendering, order-independent methods do not require complex emission-absorption integral inversion [S9].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of transfer functions in volume rendering originated with Levoy's foundational work on direct volume rendering, establishing the mapping of scalar density to color and opacity [G]. Early implementations used piecewise linear functions defined by control points [S2, unofficial]. The introduction of Programmable Transfer Functions (PTFs) on GPUs allowed for more complex, non-linear mappings and real-time interaction [S2, unofficial]. Recent developments include topology-based methods for handling complex scalar fields [S1, unofficial] and style transfer techniques for illustrative rendering [S1, 11445-illustrative-volume-rendering-with-style-transfer, unofficial].
+The concept of transfer functions in volume rendering originated with Levoy's foundational work on direct volume rendering, establishing the mapping of scalar density to color and opacity [G]. Early implementations used cubic spline interpolation defined by control points [S3, unofficial]. The introduction of Programmable Transfer Functions (PTFs) on GPUs allowed for more complex, non-linear mappings and real-time interaction [S7]. Recent developments include topology-based methods for handling complex scalar fields [S1, unofficial] and style transfer techniques for illustrative rendering [S1, unofficial].
 
